Short:        Versioned network backup with snapshots
Author:       simond@irrelevant.org (Simon Dick)
Uploader:     simond@irrelevant.org (Simon Dick)
Type:         disk/bakup
Version:      0.1
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0
Distribution: Aminet

AmiSnap is a snapshot-based backup tool for classic AmigaOS that backs
up to modern destinations - a mounted volume (any DOS device, including
smbfs/NFS mounts of a LAN NAS), and in later phases WebDAV and
S3-compatible object storage - with incremental snapshots, retention
pruning, verified restore, and full preservation of Amiga filesystem
metadata.

What generic copy-to-a-share approaches silently lose, AmiSnap
round-trips bit-perfectly: the full 32-bit protection mask (not just
HSPARWED), file comments (FileNotes), original datestamps at ticks
precision, owner/group where the filesystem provides them, and long
filenames on long-name FFS/SFS/PFS3.

The design takes the platform's CPU seriously: change detection is
metadata-first with the archive bit as a fast path, integrity hashing
(BLAKE2s) happens once per new or changed file, and encryption/TLS are
per-destination opt-ins that default to off - a trusted LAN NAS runs at
wire speed.

Snapshots are recoverable on a PC even if the Amiga is dead: the
repository format is documented and versioned, with a host-side
reference reader.

Requirements: AmigaOS 3.0+, 68020+.

This is an early development version - not yet a working backup tool.

AmiSnap is open source under the BSD 2-Clause license.
